### 2. AAuth Protocol Overview
**Dick Hardt** presented the [AAuth Protocol Overview](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/interim-2026-oauth-06/materials/slides-interim-2026-oauth-06-sessa-aauth-protocol-overview-00), highlighting how it addresses agent-oriented workflows.

* **Key Roles in AAuth:**
  * **Supervisor:** A governance/guardian agent making nondeterministic policy decisions at machine speed.
  * **Agent Provider:** Verifies agent posture and binds public keys to the agent instance (the "client").
  * **Agent:** The entity performing tasks (analogous to an OAuth client).
  * **Resource:** The target API (analogous to an OAuth Resource Server).
  * **Access Server (AS):** Manages access independently from the user (analogous to an OAuth Authorization Server).
  * **Person Server (PS):** Manages user identity and consent, functioning as a mix of an Identity Provider and a consent manager.

* **Architectural Differences from OAuth:**
  * **Cryptographic Foundation:** No pre-registration is required. Everything (requests, tokens, API calls) is cryptographically signed using HTTP Message Signatures (RFC 9421) and a dynamic discovery scheme called `Signature-Key`.
  * **Flow Inversion:** The flow starts at the Resource rather than triggering an initial redirect to the Authorization Server.
  * **Inherent Security Bindings:** Eliminates bearer tokens and authorization codes, mitigating token theft, code theft, confused deputy, and mix-up attacks.
  * **Advanced Agent Features:** Core support for budgets (limiting resource usage per token to prevent agent-driven DOS), subagents (delegated keys), call chaining, and cascading token revocation.

* **Access Modes & Deployments:**
  * Four modes of access ranging from simple API-key replacements to fully-federated flows utilizing Person Servers and Access Servers.
  * Integration mode (Proxy) allowing existing OAuth infrastructure to be wrapped inside an AAuth interface.
  * Real-world implementations include Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers, analytics services, and messaging co-ops.

